{"author":{"id":"5698b6d16d951325374257d86a2e673da0e55970c3d21c0f7c6f49018f24e5bf"},"ops":[{"type":3,"timestamp":1787164450,"nonce":"7qgpF7U1l/9z3mOl0AXMluI77Wk=","message":"**The plan is now fully specified. Three decisions taken, two clauses that need re-wording rather than implementing, and one column set the ruling did not name.**\n\n\nA code read confirms the collision comment #3 flagged is real, with a name attached: `TestAMissedTickWritesAtMostSixDigestsAndSaysWhatItDropped` (`digest_tick_test.go:629-692`) asserts that a cursor fifteen windows behind produces **six** digests for `12:40…13:30`. Under a single-number clamp with `L = 15m` and `W = 10m` it produces **two plus a tail, and logs nothing** — so after any outage longer than `L`, oto silently stops sending the digests it owed.\n\n`MaxDigestBackfill` has a recorded derivation for exactly that case (`domain/digest.go:144-179`): *\"at least half an hour of catch-up… covers a deploy, a leader election, a failover and a short database incident.\"*\n\n**Decision: keep both, and they answer different questions.**\n\n```\n  MaxDigestBackfill   how many owed WINDOWS a recovered tick still emits\n                      — outage horizon, unchanged, existing derivation stands\n  L                   how late a CASE may commit and still be counted\n                      — straggler budget, new, bounds the tail scan only\n```\n\nTaken rather than escalated because collapsing them would **delete a documented behaviour nobody ruled on**, and the standing rule on this project is to delete only what has been ruled. The conservative reading is also the smaller change.\n\n\nI wrote that the \"named constant with the gap distribution it was sized against\" clause needed relaxing. Re-reading the Done-when: that sentence is **conditional** — *\"**If the answer is a lag**, the lag is a named constant with…\"*. Bounded lookback is not a lag; it is a re-scan. The clause that applies is the second of the three offered: *\"or is counted by a later one\"*, which is exactly what the tail does. **Nothing needs relaxing.** No unmeasurable distribution is owed.\n\n\n`342e071`'s third clause says the meaninglessness of a stored `digest_window_start` without its length must be stated *where the cursor is read*, because the comment there currently argues the opposite. That can only become truthful if the row carries its own span, so `digest_covered_from` / `digest_covered_to` are part of this work.\n\n⛔ Which also corrects comment #2's claim that this \"unblocks `78388fb`\" **for free** — it does not. `DigestHeadline` (`service/digest.go:451-477`) still takes the policy's *current* window as a parameter, which is precisely the unsafe inference `342e071` is about. The span columns are what unblock it, and they are a column somebody has to add.\n\n\n- `893cee4`: *\"does no per-window query it does not need.\"* Unsatisfiable literally — **you cannot know a window is quiet without asking.** A quiet policy will always run one or two queries per tick. What the design delivers is \"one or two necessary queries\" in place of \"six redundant ones\". The clause should say that.\n- `342e071`: *\"does not produce a digest covering a span an earlier digest already covered.\"* Satisfied in effect, not in letter. Re-tiled windows are still *examined* and their spans still overlap; every Case in the overlap is already marked, so each folds to zero and **sends nothing**. The operator sees no duplicate, which is what the clause is for, but the literal reading is still violated.\n\n\nUnder the clamp it can never be non-zero, and a field that is always `0` is worse than no field. The alertable surface becomes a metric over unreported Cases instead, which is also what makes the §B.6 \"oto's quiet must be inspectable\" clause answerable in its STRONG form rather than the downgraded one: a marked-but-unreported row is *\"examined and found quiet\"*, and the absence of a row for a matched Case older than `L` is the unrecoverable gap.\n\n\nThis is ~15 files, a migration with Down assertions on both directions, a new reconcile job, and a rewrite of a test that pins existing product behaviour. I have a full implementation map (`~/.afk/oto/20260819-2150/digest-implementation-map.md`, every claim `file:line`) but **I have not read the digest code myself** — the map is a proxy, not a substitute, and the last two times I acted on a mapping pass without checking it, one claim in it was wrong.\n\nStarting a subsystem-wide change and leaving it half-done is the worse outcome; there is already one parked half-refactor on this branch (`7570090`). So this is specified, decided and ready — and deliberately not begun.","files":null}]}